strings.GNG_map_zhujin_support_tooltip_overall:
£GNG_zhujin_texticon Zhujin
--------------

The Zhujin - the common name for an emerging Cantonese-Japanese hybrid culture - are both envied and resented in equal measure. Educated and generally fluent in both Cantonese and Japanese, the Zhujin emerged from Guangdong's identity crisis as an indispensable and increasingly localized professional and bureaucratic class - the face of what Guangdong could yet become.

The Zhujin comprise [?total_national_zhu_pop_percent|2%] of Guangdong's total population.
The government has [?GNG_zhu_app|2]% support among Zhujin (Capped at [?GNG_zhu_app_cap|2]%), granting the following effects:

Monthly Corruption: {getgngzhujincorruptionpositiveornegative}
Real GDP Growth: {getgngzhujinrgdpgrowthpositiveornegative}
£GNG_chinese_texticon Chinese to £GNG_zhujin_texticon Zhujin Assimilation Rate: {getgngzhujinconversionpositiveornegative}

strings.GNG_map_chinese_support_tooltip_overall:
£GNG_chinese_texticon Chinese
--------------

Even though China no longer controls the land, the people are not so easily separated from their homeland. Ethnic Chinese make up the dominant majority across Guangdong, especially outside the cities, serving as manual labor in Guangdong's factories. Their struggle to survive as an underclass beneath the Japanese and the Zhujin showcases Guangdong as it is, rather than what it wants to be.

The Chinese comprise [?total_national_chi_pop_percent|2%] of Guangdong's total population.
The government has [?GNG_chi_app|2]% support among Chinese (Capped at [?GNG_chi_app_cap|2]%), granting the following effects:

Real GDP Growth: {getgngchinesergdpgrowthpositiveornegative}
£GNG_chinese_nation_texticon Republic of China Opinion Cap: {getgngchineserepublicofchinaopinioncap}
£GNG_chinese_texticon Chinese to £GNG_zhujin_texticon Zhujin Assimilation Rate: {getgngchineseconversionpositiveornegative}

